    "body": "# PR: Precompute `length` column to support length-grouped batching\r\n\r\n> Upstream: https://github.com/hiyouga/LLaMA-Factory (based on v0.9.5)\r\n> Title: `feat(data): precompute length column for efficient group_by_length sampling`\r\n\r\n## PR body (copy into the GitHub PR form)\r\n\r\n### Motivation\r\n\r\nIn non-packed SFT/PT training, LLaMA-Factory feeds randomly shuffled batches to the model and the collator pads every sample to the longest sequence **in the batch**. On real-world datasets with long-tailed length distributions this wastes a substantial amount of compute on padding tokens.\r\n\r\nOur measurements on the same hardware, same weights, same data (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct + LoRA, `per_device_train_batch_size=4`, `cutoff_len=1024`, datasets: `norobots`, `oasst1`, EV-charging QA, 1 epoch each):\r\n\r\n| layout | effective tokens/step | padding compute overhead | wall clock |\r\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\r\n| random batches, pad to batch-max (current default) | ~835 | ~30% | 83.3 min |\r\n| length-grouped layout (bucketed so similar lengths share a batch) | ~835 | ~0% | 37.0 min |\r\n\r\nThe full bucketed + varlen-packed layout of our scheduler reaches **2.25x wall-clock speedup (1074 vs 489 tok/s)** and +1.8 pt mean F1 over the padded-batch baseline, with the same model, data, LoRA config and seed. Length grouping is the first and cheapest layer of that gain.\r\n\r\nTransformers already ships the machinery for this: `LengthGroupedSampler` groups roughly-equal-length samples into the same batch while keeping some randomness (no curriculum bias). But today it is impractical to use in LLaMA-Factory:\r\n\r\n1. The tokenized dataset has no `length` column, so `LengthGroupedSampler` falls back to materializing `len(feature[\"input_ids\"])` for **every sample of the dataset** at startup — slow and memory-hungry;\r\n2. A `length` column added by the user would leak into the collator output and crash `model.forward`.\r\n\r\n### What this PR does\r\n\r\n1. `SupervisedDatasetProcessor.preprocess_dataset` (non-packed SFT) now emits a precomputed `length` column — the value is already known at tokenization time, so this is essentially free.\r\n2. `PretrainDatasetProcessor.preprocess_dataset` (non-packed PT) does the same.\r\n3. `MultiModalDataCollatorForSeq2Seq.__call__` pops the `length` helper column so it never reaches the model.\r\n\r\nWith this, length-grouped batching works out of the box:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# transformers >= 5.0\r\nllamafactory-cli train ... --train_sampling_strategy group_by_length\r\n\r\n# transformers 4.x\r\nllamafactory-cli train ... --group_by_length true\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `length` column is also persisted by `tokenized_path`, so cached tokenized datasets benefit too.\r\n\r\n### Notes\r\n\r\n- `LengthGroupedSampler` still shuffles (megabatch-wise), so training randomness is preserved.\r\n- No need to enable this together with `packing`: packed samples are all padded to `cutoff_len` anyway.\r\n- Eval datasets get the same column; it is dropped by the collator, so evaluation is unaffected.\r\n- Works for both map-style datasets (sampler reads `length_column_name=\"length\"` directly) — streaming mode ignores the sampler as before.\r\n\r\n### Testing\r\n\r\nVerified locally on LLaMA-Factory 0.9.5 / transformers 5.6.0: preprocessing emits the `length` column, `LengthGroupedSampler` consumes it without the slow length-materialization path, and training runs with `--train_sampling_strategy group_by_length`. Existing CPU collator tests still pass.\r\n\r\n## Files changed\r\n\r\n| file | change |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `src/llamafactory/data/processor/supervised.py` | emit `length` column in non-packed SFT preprocessing |\r\n| `src/llamafactory/data/processor/pretrain.py` | emit `length` column in non-packed PT preprocessing |\r\n| `src/llamafactory/data/collator.py` | drop `length` helper column in the collator |\r\n",
